</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The VIX closed Tuesday within the same 14 to 16 band it has occupied for most of August, even as the TSX composite closed at a record high and Ottawa confirmed retaliatory tariffs on 700 US products. The July 29 spike marks the last time the index moved meaningfully above that range.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Genuinely puzzled rather than anxious. Clients are seeing "TSX record high" and "Canada-US trade war" in the same news cycle and cannot reconcile the two. Some read the record high as confirmation everything is fine; others suspect the calm itself is the warning sign and feel uneasy without being able to name why.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The TSX hitting a record this week is real, and it is being driven by genuinely strong bank earnings, not by investors ignoring the trade war. At the same time, the VIX, which measures how much investors are paying to hedge against a market swing, has barely moved in three weeks despite the tariff escalation and the Iran sanctions news. That is not the same as the market having judged those risks and found them small. It is the market not yet having repriced them. We have two scheduled events coming, Friday''s Fed speech and the Bank of Canada''s September 2 decision, that will force that repricing one way or the other. I am not moving your portfolio ahead of either one on a guess. I am making sure we are positioned to react quickly once we know which way it breaks.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated exposure to trade-sensitive sectors, including steel, aluminum, and auto parts, where the tariff timeline is now concrete and dated.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Balanced portfolio holders with Canadian bank exposure, who are benefiting from the earnings-driven rally while also carrying the broader macro uncertainty.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients holding volatility hedges purchased earlier in the cycle, which remain inexpensive to add to while the VIX sits in the mid-teens.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client exposure to steel, aluminum, dairy, and electronics-adjacent holdings ahead of the September 8 tariff effective date</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag Friday''s Jackson Hole address and September 2 BoC decision as the two dates most likely to move the current volatility range</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check whether existing hedging positions were sized for a 14 to 16 VIX environment and whether that sizing still fits</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document any client conversations distinguishing the bank-earnings-driven TSX rally from the trade war and sanctions backdrop</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Record TSX, quiet VIX: what is and is not being priced right now<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our call. The TSX composite closed at a record 36,957.63 on Tuesday, driven by strong Canadian bank earnings, including a record quarter from Scotiabank. At the same time, the VIX closed at 15.45, well within the range it has held for most of August, despite Canada confirming retaliatory tariffs on US goods effective September 8 and a fresh round of US sanctions on Iran.<br><br>
Those two things are not contradictory, but they are not the same signal either. The earnings strength is real. The calm on the volatility side reflects a market that has not yet repriced around the tariff timeline or this week''s Fed and Bank of Canada events, not one that has assessed those risks and dismissed them.<br><br>
I will be watching Friday''s Jackson Hole address and the September 2 Bank of Canada decision closely, and I will reach out if either changes how I think about your positioning.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors reading conflicting headlines:</strong> Self-directed investors seeing "record TSX" and "trade war escalation" simultaneously often freeze rather than act, unsure which signal to trust, and have no one to walk them through the distinction.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees with concentrated bank stock holdings:</strong> Investors who have ridden the bank earnings rally without a plan for what happens if the VIX''s current anchor breaks.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners in tariff-exposed sectors:</strong> Owners of companies touching steel, aluminum, dairy, or electronics trade who are watching the September 8 deadline approach without a structured financial response.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I noticed the TSX hit a record this week at the same time Canada confirmed new tariffs against the US. Those two stories usually do not run in the same news cycle, and I am curious how you are thinking about the gap between them.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A market that has not repriced a known risk is not a market that has judged the risk to be small. It is a market waiting for a trigger. Self-directed investors have no framework for distinguishing genuine calm from an anchored range that is about to be tested, and they have no one flagging the specific dates, this Friday and September 2, when that test arrives. An advisor''s value in this window is not predicting which way the VIX breaks. It is having a positioning plan ready before it does, rather than reacting after headlines move the market.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<div class="checklist-item">When you look at a record TSX close alongside trade war headlines, how do you decide which one matters more for your portfolio?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Do you have a specific plan for September 8, when Canada''s retaliatory tariffs take effect, or for the Bank of Canada''s September 2 decision?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">How much of your current portfolio return is concentrated in the sectors that have driven this month''s rally?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Record TSX, rising trade tension: worth 15 minutes?<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
This week''s TSX record close and Canada''s newly confirmed tariffs on US goods are both real stories, and most investors are not sure how to weigh one against the other. Volatility markets have not yet repriced around either the tariff timeline or the Fed and Bank of Canada decisions landing this week and next.<br><br>
If you would find it useful to walk through what is and is not currently priced into markets, and what that means for your positioning heading into September, I would be glad to set up a short call.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
