<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The four components shown total the full $7.5 billion package. The Large Enterprise Tariff Loan facility is excluded because it is a flexibility change to an existing $10 billion facility rather than new dollars.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Incorporated business owner clients in affected sectors are feeling squeezed between two deadlines: the September 8 tariff effective date raising their costs, and an application process for relief programs they have not yet had time to evaluate. There is relief that support exists, mixed with frustration at not knowing which of five programs actually fits their situation.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Ottawa announced $7.5 billion in tariff support on Tuesday, and for your corporation specifically, two pieces are worth evaluating right away. If you are a larger operation with sustained tariff exposure, the Large Enterprise Tariff Loan facility now offers 36 months of liquidity support and terms up to 15 years, both meaningfully longer than before. If you are a smaller incorporated business, BDC''s Pivot to Grow program has a new $500 million stream with interest-only payments for three years, and the revenue threshold to qualify just dropped to $1 million. There is also non-repayable money available through the Regional Tariff Response Initiative starting in September, which does not sit on your balance sheet as debt the way the loan programs do. I want to walk through your corporation''s specific tariff exposure so we can map it against these five programs before the September 8 tariffs take effect.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> CCPC clients whose corporations directly import or export goods in the newly tariffed categories, including steel, aluminum, dairy, appliances, agricultural equipment, pulp and paper, and electronics.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Business owner clients with indirect supply chain exposure, who likely qualify only for the broader Regional Tariff Response Initiative or Diversification Fund rather than the sector-specific programs.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Smaller incorporated clients previously excluded from BDC tariff programs by the higher revenue threshold, who now qualify at the $1 million level.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify which corporate clients have direct exposure to the tariff categories affected by the September 8 counter-tariffs</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For CCPC clients under $1 million in prior-year revenue, flag the newly lowered BDC eligibility threshold</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Compare the debt-service implications of the LETL facility''s new 36-month, 15-year terms against each client''s existing corporate financing</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare liquidity-needs documentation ahead of the Regional Tariff Response Initiative''s September effective date</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The $7.5B tariff support package and what applies to your corporation<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following our conversation, here is the summary of Tuesday''s $7.5 billion federal tariff support package as it applies to your situation.<br><br>
The Large Enterprise Tariff Loan facility now offers up to 36 months of liquidity support and 15-year loan terms, both extended from the prior 24 months and 10 years. BDC''s Pivot to Grow program has a new $500 million stream with loans from $250,000 to $5 million on interest-only terms for three years, and the eligibility threshold dropped to $1 million in annual revenue. The Regional Tariff Response Initiative''s non-repayable contribution cap rises to $3 million in September and can now cover liquidity needs directly.<br><br>
Based on your corporation''s exposure, I recommend we evaluate [specific program] first. Let''s set up time to review the application requirements before the September 8 tariff date.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Incorporated business owners in tariff-exposed sectors:</strong> Owners of CCPCs touching steel, aluminum, dairy, appliances, agricultural equipment, pulp and paper, or electronics trade, who need to evaluate five new or enhanced federal programs against their specific financing structure.</p>
<p><strong>Small business owners newly eligible for BDC support:</strong> Owners of corporations between $1 million and the prior, higher revenue threshold, who were excluded from tariff-related BDC programs until Tuesday''s announcement.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners without incorporated tax planning in place:</strong> Owners operating without a CCPC structure or without recent corporate cash flow projections, who are least prepared to document a liquidity-needs case for these programs.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Ottawa put together a $7.5 billion tariff support package this week with real financing terms attached, longer loan terms, lower eligibility thresholds, and new non-repayable money, and I am curious whether anyone has walked you through which parts actually apply to your business.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A business owner managing tariff exposure without an advisor is left comparing five federal programs against their own balance sheet with no framework for which one fits. The distinction between repayable financing, like the Large Enterprise Tariff Loan facility or BDC''s Pivot to Grow, and non-repayable contributions, like the Regional Tariff Response Initiative, has direct implications for how a corporation structures its debt-to-equity position this year. An advisor who has already mapped these five programs against different exposure profiles can shorten what would otherwise be weeks of independent research into a single focused conversation.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Does your corporation have direct exposure to the sectors covered by Canada''s new retaliatory tariffs effective September 8?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Has anyone reviewed your eligibility for the newly enhanced BDC or Large Enterprise Tariff Loan programs?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">How would an extended 36-month liquidity window or a 15-year loan term change your current financing structure?</div>
</div>
