<script>(function(){
var _cs = document.currentScript;
function el(tag, attrs, txt){
  var e = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", tag);
  for (var k in attrs) e.setAttribute(k, attrs[k]);
  if (txt !== undefined) e.textContent = txt;
  return e;
}
var svg = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","svg");
svg.setAttribute("viewBox","0 0 680 300");
svg.setAttribute("width","100%");

var margin = {left:62, top:18, right:24, bottom:46};
var PW = 594, PH = 300 - margin.top - margin.bottom;

var labels = ["Jul 27","Jul 28","Jul 29","Jul 30","Jul 31","Aug 4","Aug 5","Aug 6","Aug 7","Aug 10","Aug 11","Aug 12","Aug 13","Aug 14","Aug 17","Aug 18","Aug 19","Aug 20","Aug 21","Aug 24","Aug 25"];
var data = [3.557,3.531,3.597,3.590,3.665,3.549,3.561,3.626,3.643,3.721,3.708,3.692,3.626,3.678,3.722,3.697,3.694,3.741,3.760,3.690,3.650];
var n = data.length;

var yMin = 3.45, yMax = 3.85;
var xp = function(i){ return margin.left + i*(PW/(n-1)); };
var yp = function(v){ return margin.top + PH*(yMax-v)/(yMax-yMin); };

for (var g=3.5; g<=3.8; g+=0.1){
  svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:margin.left, x2:margin.left+PW, y1:yp(g), y2:yp(g), stroke:"#ececec","stroke-width":"0.5"}));
}

// event marker: Aug 21 two-year plus high (index 18)
var evX = xp(18);
svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:evX, x2:evX, y1:margin.top, y2:margin.top+PH, stroke:"#1a3560", "stroke-opacity":"0.5","stroke-dasharray":"2,3"}));
svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:evX-3, y:margin.top+12, "text-anchor":"end","font-size":"7","font-weight":"700",fill:"#1a3560","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}, "26-MONTH HIGH"));

var d = "";
for (var i=0;i<n;i++){
  d += (i===0?"M":"L") + xp(i) + "," + yp(data[i]) + " ";
}
svg.appendChild(el("path",{d:d, fill:"none", stroke:"#4a5568","stroke-width":"1.6"}));

svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:margin.left, x2:margin.left+PW, y1:margin.top+PH, y2:margin.top+PH, stroke:"#d8d8d8","stroke-width":"1"}));

var lastX = xp(n-1), lastY = yp(data[n-1]);
svg.appendChild(el("circle",{cx:lastX, cy:lastY, r:4, fill:"#4a5568"}));

[3.5,3.6,3.7,3.8].forEach(function(v){
  svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:margin.left-6, y:yp(v)+3, "text-anchor":"end","font-size":"8.5",fill:"#aaaaaa","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}, v.toFixed(1)+"%"));
});

[0,4,9,14,18,20].forEach(function(i){
  svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:xp(i), y:margin.top+PH+16, "text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"8",fill:"#999999","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}, labels[i]));
});

var pillText = data[n-1].toFixed(2)+"%";
var pillW = Math.ceil(pillText.length*9*0.58)+10;
var pillH = 16;
var pillX = lastX - pillW - 6;
if (pillX < margin.left) pillX = margin.left;
var pillY = lastY - pillH/2;
svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:pillX, y:pillY, width:pillW, height:pillH, rx:3, fill:"#e8a825"}));
svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:pillX+pillW/2, y:pillY+pillH/2+4, "text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"9","font-weight":"700",fill:"#111111","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}, pillText));

_cs.parentNode.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Investing.com, Trading Economics, Aug 25, 2026. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The yield eased for a fourth straight session after touching 3.76% on August 21, its highest level since April 2024, as trade war escalation lowered the probability markets assign to a Bank of Canada hike this year.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with fixed income or GIC-heavy portfolios are noticing yields moving and want to know if this is the start of a rate-cut cycle or a temporary wobble. Clients with variable-rate mortgages up for renewal are anxious about whether September 2 brings relief or not. Both groups are reading the same headline differently.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The 10-year Government of Canada yield has come down about 10 basis points since peaking at 3.76% on August 21, and the reason matters more than the direction. July''s jobs and inflation numbers were actually strong, the kind of data that normally pushes yields up, not down. What is pulling yields lower instead is the trade war with the US, which escalated into confirmed retaliatory tariffs the same week yields turned. The bond market is telling us that trade risk is currently outweighing strong domestic data in how it expects the Bank of Canada to act. The Bank''s own decision on September 2 will tell us whether Governor Macklem agrees with that read. I am not making portfolio changes based on a guess about that outcome, but I am watching it closely because it will affect both bond positioning and mortgage renewal timing.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with mortgages renewing in the next two quarters, where the September 2 decision directly affects their refinancing rate.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Fixed income holders who benefit from yield declines on existing bond positions but face lower reinvestment rates on maturing holdings.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Dividend-paying equity holders, since falling yields typically make equity income relatively more attractive against bond alternatives.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients with mortgage renewals in the September to December window for a September 2 rate decision follow-up</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review fixed income duration positioning against the possibility of continued yield declines through year end</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm which clients are asking about GIC laddering given the yield''s recent retreat from its 26-month high</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document the distinction between the July data''s hawkish signal and the trade war''s dovish signal in client files</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Why bond yields are falling despite strong July data<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following our call, here is the short version of what is happening in bond markets. The Government of Canada 10-year yield closed at 3.65% on Tuesday, down from a 26-month high of 3.76% on August 21.<br><br>
The interesting part is why. July''s jobs and inflation data were both strong, which normally pushes yields higher. Instead, yields have fallen for four straight sessions because the US-Canada trade war escalated into confirmed retaliatory tariffs over the same window. The bond market is pricing trade-related growth risk as the more important factor right now.<br><br>
The Bank of Canada''s rate decision on September 2 will tell us whether the Bank agrees. I will follow up as soon as we have that outcome.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors confused by conflicting rate signals:</strong> Self-directed investors seeing strong jobs data and falling bond yields in the same week often do not understand which force is winning, or why it matters for their portfolio.</p>
<p><strong>Homeowners with upcoming mortgage renewals:</strong> Borrowers renewing in the next six months who have not connected the September 2 Bank of Canada decision to their specific renewal timeline.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees managing GIC and bond ladders:</strong> Fixed income investors deciding whether to lock in current yields or wait, without a framework for reading the trade war''s effect on the rate path.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Bond yields fell for four straight sessions even though July''s jobs and inflation numbers were strong, and the reason is the trade war with the US, not the domestic data. I am curious whether you have a plan for the September 2 Bank of Canada decision or your mortgage renewal in light of that.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>Self-directed investors and unadvised homeowners are working from headlines that often contradict each other, strong jobs data one week, falling yields the next, without a way to reconcile the two. An advisor''s value here is explaining which signal the market is actually weighting more heavily right now, and translating that into a specific action, whether that is GIC ladder timing, mortgage renewal strategy, or fixed income duration, rather than leaving the client to guess.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
