<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">WTI has fallen in three of the last four sessions since Washington''s expanded Iran sanctions campaign, a decline that only makes sense once the sanctions are read against the diplomatic track running in parallel.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with concentrated energy exposure are anxious watching Canadian Natural and Suncor decline even as headlines describe the toughest Iran sanctions yet. Clients without energy exposure are largely unaware oil is falling and unaware of what it might mean for their broader cost of living and portfolio.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Oil has fallen for three straight sessions even though Washington just announced its toughest Iran sanctions to date, and the reason is that the sanctions stopped short of targeting Iran''s trading partners directly, combined with real diplomatic progress on a Strait of Hormuz shipping corridor. That is actually good news for the broader Canadian economy, since lower oil prices reduce one of the pressures on inflation the Bank of Canada has been watching. It is not good news for your energy holdings specifically, since Canadian energy names are down on the same news. I want to make sure your portfolio reflects the fact that these two effects run in opposite directions rather than assuming they cancel out evenly.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated Canadian energy sector exposure, including direct holdings in Suncor, Canadian Natural, Imperial Oil, or Cenovus.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Diversified TSX index holders, who are net insulated since the broader index hit a record high on financials and materials strength the same session energy declined.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients sensitive to household fuel and transport costs, and fixed income holders positioned for the Bank of Canada to weigh a softer inflation input at its September 2 decision.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review energy sector weighting for clients with concentrated Canadian Natural, Suncor, Imperial Oil, or Cenovus positions</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag the diplomatic corridor talks between Iran and Oman as the development most likely to extend or reverse this week''s oil decline</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the softer oil input as a potential factor in the Bank of Canada''s September 2 inflation assessment</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document the distinction between the sanctions headline and the market''s actual price reaction for client files</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Why oil fell despite the toughest Iran sanctions yet<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following our call, here is the summary of what has been driving oil prices this week. WTI crude fell to $80.78 Wednesday, its third straight down session, even though the US Treasury announced its most sweeping Iran sanctions campaign to date on Monday.<br><br>
The sanctions stopped short of targeting Iran''s trading partners directly, and diplomatic talks between Iran and Oman on a Strait of Hormuz shipping corridor have progressed. Together, these have eased the supply-disruption fear that had been keeping oil elevated.<br><br>
Canadian energy names, including Suncor and Canadian Natural, are down on this news, while the broader TSX hit a record high on financials strength the same session. I am watching both the diplomatic track and your specific energy exposure closely.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors holding concentrated energy positions:</strong> Self-directed investors watching Canadian energy stocks decline on what looks like bullish geopolitical news, with no framework for why the market is reacting the way it is.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees sensitive to inflation and cost of living:</strong> Investors who have not connected falling oil prices to reduced pressure on the Bank of Canada''s September 2 inflation assessment.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with energy sector or transport cost exposure:</strong> Owners whose input costs are directly affected by oil prices and who lack a structured view on where the Hormuz situation is heading.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Oil has fallen three straight sessions despite Washington''s toughest Iran sanctions ever, and most investors are reading that as a contradiction rather than understanding the actual mechanism behind it. I am curious how your portfolio is positioned for whichever way the Strait of Hormuz situation breaks next.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed investor watching a geopolitical headline and a market reaction that appear to contradict each other has no framework for resolving that contradiction, and often makes a portfolio decision based on the headline rather than the actual chain of consequence. An advisor''s value in this specific moment is tracing that chain, from the sanctions'' actual scope, to the parallel diplomatic corridor talks, to the split effect on Canadian energy names versus the broader economy, and translating it into a concrete portfolio position rather than a guess.</p>
</div>
