  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients seeing "TSX record high" in a headline feel reassured, sometimes overly so, without knowing the gain was concentrated in two sectors. Clients with concentrated energy holdings may feel confused or anxious that their positions are down on a day the index as a whole is celebrated as a record.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The TSX hit a record close Tuesday, but the gain was concentrated in financials and materials, not spread across the index. Scotiabank posted a record quarter and BMO grew earnings 22%, and both stocks rallied hard. Energy moved the opposite direction the same day because oil fell for a third straight session. If your portfolio is diversified across the index, Tuesday was a genuinely good day. If you are more concentrated in energy specifically, the record headline does not reflect what happened in your positions, and I want to make sure we are looking at your actual exposure, not just the index number.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated Canadian bank holdings, who benefited directly from Tuesday''s earnings-driven rally, and clients with concentrated energy holdings, who did not.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Broadly diversified TSX index holders, who saw a genuine net gain but should understand its composition.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients considering adding to bank exposure ahead of Thursday''s RBC, TD, and CIBC reports, given the sector''s four-quarter beat streak.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
